1. Understand what we are creating

Your setup will look like this:

Windows
   |
   | WSL Ubuntu
   |
   |---- Ansible Control Node
   |
   | SSH using private key
   |
   ↓
AWS EC2 Ubuntu
   |
   |---- Managed Node

=> Control Node

Your WSL Ubuntu will be the control node.

It will run commands such as:

=> Managed Node

Your AWS EC2 Ubuntu server will be the managed node.

Ansible will connect to it through SSH.

A) PART 1 — Check WSL
Step 1: Open WSL

Open Windows PowerShell or Command Prompt and run:

wsl

You should get something similar to:

bilal@DESKTOP:~$

Now check your Linux version:

cat /etc/os-release

You should see Ubuntu information.

Also check your IP:

ip addr

For this lab, you don't need to worry much about the WSL IP.

B) PART 2 — Install SSH and Ansible
Step 2: Update packages

Inside WSL:

sudo apt update

Then:

sudo apt upgrade -y

=> Step 3: Check SSH

Run:

ssh -V

You should see something like:

OpenSSH_9.x

If SSH is not installed:

sudo apt install openssh-client -y

=> Step 4: Install Ansible

Run:

sudo apt install ansible -y

Check:

ansible --version

You should get something similar to:

ansible [core ...]
  python version = ...

Now your WSL machine can act as an Ansible Control Node.

=> PART 3 — AWS EC2

You need one Linux EC2 instance.

For this lab, Ubuntu is easiest.

For example:

OS: Ubuntu
Instance: Any suitable small instance
Username: ubuntu
SSH Port: 22

Your EC2 Security Group must allow:

Type: SSH
Port: 22
Source: Your IP address

You will need the EC2:

Public IPv4 address

For example:

13.234.xxx.xxx

=> PART 4 — First SSH Connection

Before setting up Ansible, make sure normal SSH works.

Suppose your EC2 private key is:

my-ec2-key.pem

You need to make this key available inside WSL.

If your .pem file is in Windows Downloads, you can access it from WSL approximately like:

/mnt/c/Users/<WindowsUsername>/Downloads/my-ec2-key.pem

Copy it into your WSL home directory:

cp /mnt/c/Users/<WindowsUsername>/Downloads/my-ec2-key.pem ~/

Then:

chmod 400 ~/my-ec2-key.pem

Why chmod 400?

It makes the private key readable only by you.

=> PART 5 — Test SSH Manually

Suppose your EC2 IP is:

13.234.56.78

Run:

ssh -i ~/my-ec2-key.pem ubuntu@13.234.56.78

If everything is correct, you should enter the EC2 server.

You might see:

ubuntu@ip-172-31-xx-xx:~$

That means:

WSL
 ↓ SSH
EC2

is working.

Exit:

exit

=> PART 6 — Generate Your Own SSH Key

Now we will create the key shown in your diagram.

On WSL:

ssh-keygen

You will see:

Generating public/private rsa key pair.
Enter file in which to save the key:

Just press Enter.

Then:

Enter passphrase:

For this lab, press Enter twice to leave it empty.

You should get:

Your identification has been saved in /home/bilal/.ssh/id_rsa
Your public key has been saved in /home/bilal/.ssh/id_rsa.pub

Check:

ls -l ~/.ssh/

You should see:

id_rsa
id_rsa.pub
Important

These are two different files:

id_rsa

= Private key

id_rsa.pub

= Public key

Never share:

id_rsa

The public key is the one we put on the EC2 server.

=> PART 7 — Understand Public and Private Keys

Think of it like this:

WSL Control Node

Private Key
id_rsa
   🔑
   |
   | SSH authentication
   ↓
EC2

Public Key
id_rsa.pub
   🔓
   |
   ↓
~/.ssh/authorized_keys

The EC2 server stores your public key.

Your WSL machine keeps the private key.

SSH uses them together to authenticate you.

=> PART 8 — Put Public Key on EC2

There are two ways.

For your AWS EC2 lab, I recommend the first method because EC2 normally gives you the initial .pem key.

Method 1 — Using the existing .pem key

From WSL:

cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub

You will see something like:

ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2EAAAADAQABAAABAQ... bilal@computer

Copy the entire line.

Now SSH into EC2 using your original .pem:

ssh -i ~/my-ec2-key.pem ubuntu@13.234.56.78

On EC2:

mkdir -p ~/.ssh

Then:

nano ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

Paste your WSL public key into this file.

Save and exit.

Then:

Then:

chmod 700 ~/.ssh
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

Exit EC2:

exit

=> PART 9 — Test Passwordless SSH

Now comes the important part.

From WSL, try:

ssh ubuntu@13.234.56.78

Notice something:

We are not using:

-i my-ec2-key.pem

If everything is configured correctly, SSH should connect without asking for the EC2 password.

Why?

Because SSH automatically looks for your key in:

~/.ssh/id_rsa

and uses it.

You should enter the EC2 server.

ubuntu@ip-172-31-xx-xx:~$

Congratulations — you have configured passwordless SSH authentication.

